## 10. Minimal Port

The platform boundary exposes only:

- bounded, stable scoped-file enumeration plus read and atomic
  compare-and-swap write, all with content hashes;
- bounded, cancellable command execution without a shell and with a structured
  receipt;
- one LLM proposal call through an explicitly bound provider interface.

An unavailable capability is `UNSUPPORTED`, not a semantic fallback. Tool
receipts include operation id, status, timing, scope/argv, exit state, output
digests, truncation flags, and before/after hashes where applicable.

Enumeration rejects path escape, symlinks/junctions, non-regular files, and
count/depth budget overflow. Its receipt is path-sorted and binds every file to
its SHA-256 and byte size. An LLM call returns a `PROPOSED` receipt binding the
canonical request, provider/model identity, provider result digest, and the
proposal; the Port never interprets or executes the proposed action.

Adapter support is declared by a JSON-compatible
`yuan.adapter-descriptor/v1`. Each required capability is explicit. A
`supported` adapter binds a versioned executable Port, its SHA-256, and a
conformance entrypoint. An `unsupported` adapter names the missing executable
binding and exposes no fallback Port. Platform documentation is not support
evidence.

Trusting an executable is insufficient: every command binds an invocation
profile. The reference Python profile accepts only isolated `-c`, rejects
absolute arguments outside the Port root, and installs an audit sandbox that
denies filesystem writes, subprocesses, sockets, ctypes, and OS mutation.
Unprofiled executables are rejected.

